    "KPA-40 on default harness DEM (2 m): the shortest axis-aligned valley that can yield a keypoint is 7 cells = 12.0 m (predicted 2 * MIN_REACH_CELLS + 1 = 7 cells). There is no metric floor any more: once a valley starts at its divide it is tens of cells long before it is a channel at all.",
    "Window sizing (a TerrainFlow convention, sized on SYNTHETIC correlated roughness \u2014 no published source supplies one): spreads across roughness_m 0.00-0.10 on the trunk keyline are 10m 0.0206 (1.03x MIN_SLOPE_EASE); 20m 0.0115 (0.57x MIN_SLOPE_EASE); 50m 0.0111 (0.56x MIN_SLOPE_EASE). Shortest window whose spread stays under MIN_SLOPE_EASE: 20m.",
    "Roughness sweep, roughness_m 0.00 -> 0.25: primary valleys 6 -> 71 -> 68 -> 62; valleys the fit could be run on 6 -> 71 -> 68 -> 62; valleys clearing the 2% bar 6 -> 68 -> 64 -> 59. (Before KPA-52 closed, noise shattered the channel network into stubs that the 35 m floor refused, so nothing cleared the bar at any roughness; the floor is gone and every valley is fitted, so the third series is now the one that says whether noise fabricates keypoints.)"
